cartridge "DISINTEGRATOR/B.SCHULAK/1985"

' DISINTEGRATOR
' A ColecoVision game based on "Disintegrator" by Barbara Schulak, Ahoy!
' August 1985 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, 1,824 bytes, loaded
' and started by the one-line DSNTGRTOR BOOT). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/disintegrator.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a starry night over red desert ground, eight domed silos,
' a magenta alien pod as a smooth two-colour sprite, missiles with a
' flame, blasts, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: alien pods drop one at a time from the sky above one of your
' eight underground silos. Launch a missile from the silo under the pod
' before it touches down. Fifteen pods destroyed clear a level; each level
' they fall faster. A pod that reaches the silos costs one of three lives.
' Clear level 9 to save the silos.
'
' From the original (orig/): each pod falls straight down from the top
' above a silo picked at random, never the same silo twice in a row; a
' missile launched from that silo always meets it, a launch from another
' silo does nothing (the C64 ignored the other keys); 10 points a pod (the
' C64 counted three digits and printed a fixed 0 after them); 15 pods a
' level, a pod touching the silos (the C64's sprite-background collision)
' costs a life and the hit count carries on; 3 lives; the fall delay starts
' at 9 and loses 1 a level, the game ending after level 9 (delay 0); the
' fall speed from the C64 step loop's cycles: a pod falls one pixel per
' step of about 1290 x delay + 200 cycles, on a 60 Hz C64 with about
' 15,600 free cycles a frame 1.32 pixels a frame at level 1 (SpdT); the
' 107-pixel drop (C64 Y 50 down to the silo row) is kept: Y 16 to 123.
'
' Liberties: the stick picks the silo (a marker slides under it, a step
' every 3 frames when held) and a button launches, instead of the number
' keys 1-8; a missile from the wrong silo flies up and misses, and only
' one missile is in the air at a time, so sweeping and firing at random
' wastes time; the missile flies 6 pixels a frame (the C64's crept one
' pixel a step as the pod still fell); from level 6 the C64's speed (3 to
' 10 pixels a frame, faster than anyone can react with a stick) is capped
' at 2.5 to 2.8 pixels a frame (SpdT); a blast where a pod touches down
' and a single border flash (the C64 cycled the border colours); a row of
' 15 markers shows the pods left in the level; the wave counter starts
' again on a new level as on the C64; no pause key (the C64's space bar);
' ALL SILOS SAVED or GAME OVER then the title (the C64 asked PLAY AGAIN
' Y/N); the title letters appear one by one with a tick, as on the C64.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick (left and right) chooses the
' silo, either button launches. On the title, up and down choose, a
' button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

